INSERT INTO `ai_chats` (`id`, `user_email`, `sender`, `message`, `image_url`, `created_at`) VALUES
(33, 'vdholariya623@rku.ac.in', 'ai', '## 1. TRIP OVERVIEW<br />\n| Item | Details |<br />\n|------|---------|<br />\n| <strong>Destination</strong> | Mumbai, Maharashtra, India |<br />\n| <strong>Duration</strong> | 1 day (full‑day city immersion) |<br />\n| <strong>Best travel period</strong> | <strong>October – February</strong> – pleasant 20‑30 °C, low humidity |<br />\n| <strong>Estimated budget</strong> | <strong>Luxury:</strong> ≈ ₹30,000‑₹45,000 / ≈ US$360‑540  &lt;br&gt; <strong>Mid‑range:</strong> ≈ ₹12,000‑₹18,000 / ≈ US$145‑220  &lt;br&gt; <strong>Budget:</strong> ≈ ₹5,000‑₹8,000 / ≈ US$60‑95 |<br />\n| <strong>Travel style</strong> | Choose one of the three tiers above – the itinerary works for all, with optional upgrades for transport, dining and accommodation. |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 2. DESTINATION HIGHLIGHTS<br />\n- <strong>Iconic landmarks</strong> – Gateway of India, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (CST), Marine Drive “Queen’s Necklace”, Haji Ali Dargah.  <br />\n- <strong>Cultural sites</strong> – Kala Ghoda arts district, Prince of Wales Museum, Jehangir Art Gallery.  <br />\n- <strong>Wellness &amp; relaxation</strong> – Spa at The Taj Mahal Palace, yoga session at a beachfront studio on Marine Drive.  <br />\n- <strong>Local experiences</strong> – Street‑food crawl in Girgaon (vada pav, pav bhaji), ferry ride to Elephanta Island (if time permits), Bollywood‑themed photo‑stop at Film City (optional).  <br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 3. DAY‑BY‑DAY ITINERARY  <br />\n*(All times are approximate and can be shifted according to your preferred pace.)*<br />\n<br />\n| Time | Activity | Details &amp; Transfer |<br />\n|------|----------|--------------------|<br />\n| <strong>07:30 – 08:30</strong> | <strong>Breakfast &amp; check‑in</strong> | Arrive at <strong>The Taj Mahal Palace</strong> (Colaba) – drop luggage, enjoy a classic Indian breakfast at <strong>Sea Lounge</strong> (est. ₹1,200 / US$15). |<br />\n| <strong>08:45 – 10:00</strong> | <strong>Gateway of India</strong> | 5‑minute walk from hotel. Photo stop; optional short ferry to <strong>Elephanta Caves</strong> (requires 2 h, see “Optional” below). |<br />\n| <strong>10:15 – 11:30</strong> | <strong>CST (Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus)</strong> | Take a quick 10‑min cab/auto to CST. Explore the Victorian‑Gothic architecture (guided audio tour on app, free). |<br />\n| <strong>11:45 – 12:30</strong> | <strong>Lunch – Seafood</strong> | Walk to <strong>Trishna</strong> (Marine Lines) – famed for butter garlic crab (≈ ₹1,800 / US$22). |<br />\n| <strong>12:45 – 13:30</strong> | <strong>Marine Drive promenade</strong> | Stroll north along the “Queen’s Necklace”, enjoy sea breeze; optional quick yoga session at <strong>The Yoga Studio</strong> (₹800 / US$10). |<br />\n| <strong>13:45 – 15:00</strong> | <strong>Haji Ali Dargah</strong> | 15‑min drive (taxi/auto). Visit the iconic shrine on a tiny islet; respect dress code (shoulders &amp; knees covered). |<br />\n| <strong>15:30 – 16:30</strong> | <strong>Kala Ghoda Arts Precinct</strong> | 10‑min drive to this vibrant heritage lane. Browse galleries, street art, and boutique craft shops. |<br />\n| <strong>16:45 – 18:00</strong> | <strong>Afternoon tea / snack</strong> | <strong>Leopold Café</strong> (Colaba) – historic café, try the famous <strong>Bombay sandwich</strong> (≈ ₹300 / US$4). |<br />\n| <strong>18:15 – 20:00</strong> | <strong>Sunset at Marine Drive</strong> | Return to Marine Drive for sunset; grab a fresh <strong>coconut water</strong> from a stall (≈ ₹150 / US$2). |<br />\n| <strong>20:15 – 22:30</strong> | <strong>Dinner – Fine dining</strong> | <strong>The Table</strong> (Colaba) – contemporary global cuisine with Indian twists (₹3,500 / US$42 per person, includes wine). |<br />\n| <strong>22:45</strong> | <strong>Return to hotel / nightcap</strong> | End the day with a nightcap at the hotel’s <strong>Bar</strong> or simply relax in your room. |<br />\n<br />\n### Optional: Elephanta Caves (adds ~3 h)<br />\n- <strong>08:45 – 09:00</strong>: Board ferry from Gateway (₹300 / US$4).  <br />\n- <strong>09:30 – 11:30</strong>: Explore rock‑cut temples (guided audio guide, ₹200 / US$2).  <br />\n- <strong>12:00</strong>: Return to Gateway and continue itinerary (skip CST &amp; Marine Drive if pressed for time).  <br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 4. ACCOMMODATION  <br />\n| Property (Verified) | Location | Facilities | Approx. Price (per night) |<br />\n|----------------------|----------|------------|---------------------------|<br />\n| <strong>The Taj Mahal Palace</strong> | Colaba, near Gateway | Luxury spa, rooftop pool, 5‑star dining, 24 h concierge | <strong>₹25,000‑₹35,000</strong> (US$300‑$420) – *estimate* |<br />\n| <strong>Trident, Nariman Point</strong> | Business district, sea view | Rooftop pool, fitness centre, multiple restaurants | <strong>₹12,000‑₹18,000</strong> (US$145‑$220) – *estimate* |<br />\n| <strong>Hotel Suba Palace</strong> | Colaba | Clean rooms, rooftop terrace, free Wi‑Fi | <strong>₹5,500‑₹7,500</strong> (US$66‑$90) – *estimate* |<br />\n<br />\n*Choose the tier that matches your budget; all are real, reputable hotels with confirmed locations.*<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 5. FOOD AND EXPERIENCES  <br />\n| Meal | Recommendation | Why it’s special |<br />\n|------|----------------|------------------|<br />\n| <strong>Breakfast</strong> | <strong>Sea Lounge</strong> (Taj) – masala omelette, fresh fruit, masala chai | Elegant setting with sea view |<br />\n| <strong>Lunch</strong> | <strong>Trishna</strong> – butter garlic crab, pomfret fry | Iconic seafood house, praised by chefs worldwide |<br />\n| <strong>Snack</strong> | <strong>Leopold Café</strong> – Bombay sandwich, chai | Historic hangout of the 1940s, lively atmosphere |<br />\n| <strong>Dinner</strong> | <strong>The Table</strong> – global‑fusion menu, extensive wine list | Contemporary fine‑dining with creative plating |<br />\n| <strong>Street‑food</strong> | <strong>Girgaon Chowpatty</strong> – vada pav, bhel puri (evening) | Authentic Mumbai street flavours |<br />\n<br />\n<strong>Cultural tip:</strong> When dining at religious sites (e.g., Haji Ali), remove shoes and dress modestly.<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 6. ESTIMATED BUDGET  <br />\n<br />\n| Category | Luxury | Mid‑range | Budget |<br />\n|----------|--------|-----------|--------|<br />\n| <strong>Accommodation (1 night)</strong> | ₹30,000 | ₹15,000 | ₹6,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Transport (taxi/auto, ferry)</strong> | ₹2,500 | ₹1,500 | ₹800 |<br />\n| <strong>Food &amp; drinks</strong> | ₹8,000 | ₹4,500 | ₹2,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Activities (entry, guide, yoga)</strong> | ₹2,000 | ₹1,200 | ₹500 |<br />\n| <strong>Miscellaneous (tips, souvenirs)</strong> | ₹1,500 | ₹800 | ₹300 |<br />\n| <strong>Total (approx.)</strong> | <strong>₹44,000</strong> (US$530) | <strong>₹22,000</strong> (US$265) | <strong>₹10,600</strong> (US$128) |<br />\n*All figures are <strong>estimates</strong> based on 2024 rates and may vary with season, availability and personal choices.*<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 7. IMPORTANT TRAVEL TIPS  <br />\n<br />\n- <strong>Weather &amp; clothing:</strong> Light cotton or linen for day; a light sweater for evenings (especially near the sea). Comfortable walking shoes are a must.  <br />\n- <strong>Transport:</strong> Use <strong>Uber/Ola</strong> for reliable cabs; auto‑rickshaws are cheap but negotiate fare beforehand. The <strong>Mumbai Metro</strong> (Line 1) connects key points (e.g., CST to Churchgate).  <br />\n- <strong>Reservations:</strong> <strong>Hotel</strong> and <strong>fine‑dining dinner</strong> (The Table) should be booked at least 2 days in advance, especially during peak season.  <br />\n- <strong>Safety:</strong> Mumbai is generally safe for tourists, but keep an eye on belongings in crowded areas and avoid isolated beaches after dark.  <br />\n- <strong>Documents:</strong> Carry a <strong>photocopy of your passport/ID</strong>, a <strong>printed itinerary</strong>, and a <strong>digital copy</strong> on your phone. Indian visa (e‑Visa) must be valid for the entire stay.  <br />\n- <strong>Health:</strong> Drink bottled water only; carry a small bottle of hand sanitizer. If you have dietary restrictions, inform restaurant staff—most upscale venues accommodate.  <br />\n- <strong>Currency:</strong> Indian Rupee (₹). ATMs are widely available; most hotels accept major credit cards, but keep some cash for street vendors.  <br />\n- <strong>Connectivity:</strong> Purchase a <strong>local SIM</strong> (e.g., Jio, Airtel) at the airport for cheap data (≈ ₹300 for 10 GB).  <br />\n<br />\nEnjoy your whirlwind day in Mumbai! If you’d like a deeper dive into any segment (e.g., a full‑day Elephanta tour or a Bollywood‑themed experience), just let me know. Safe travels.', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1476514525535-07fb3b4ae5f1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80', '2026-11-19 08:10:53'),
(34, 'vdholariya623@rku.ac.in', 'user', 'plan a mumbai trip of 1 day', NULL, '2026-11-19 08:13:41'),
(35, 'vdholariya623@rku.ac.in', 'ai', '## 1️⃣ TRIP OVERVIEW  <br />\n<br />\n| Item | Details |<br />\n|------|---------|<br />\n| <strong>Destination</strong> | Mumbai, Maharashtra, India |<br />\n| <strong>Duration</strong> | 1 day (full‑day city immersion) |<br />\n| <strong>Best travel period</strong> | October – March (pleasant 15‑30 °C, low humidity) |<br />\n| <strong>Estimated budget</strong> | <strong>₹ 7,000 – ₹ 12,000</strong> per person (≈ US$ 85‑150) – see breakdown below |<br />\n| <strong>Travel style</strong> | Urban luxury‑comfort with a dash of local culture |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 2️⃣ DESTINATION HIGHLIGHTS  <br />\n<br />\n- <strong>Iconic attractions</strong> – Gateway of India, Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus (UNESCO), Marine Drive “Queen’s Necklace”, and the historic Kala Ghoda art precinct.  <br />\n- <strong>Landscapes &amp; landmarks</strong> – Sunset over the Arabian Sea from Marine Drive, panoramic city view from the <strong>Elephanta Caves</strong> (short ferry ride).  <br />\n- <strong>Wellness experiences</strong> – Ayurvedic spa at <strong>The Taj Mahal Palace</strong> or a quick yoga session at <strong>Madhavendra Yoga Centre</strong> (Marine Drive).  <br />\n- <strong>Local activities</strong> – Street‑food tasting in <strong>Crawford Market</strong>, a heritage walk through <strong>South Bombay</strong>, and a quick shopping stop at <strong>Colaba Causeway</strong> for designer‑boutique finds or traditional handicrafts.<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 3️⃣ DAY‑BY‑DAY ITINERARY  <br />\n<br />\n### Morning  <br />\n<br />\n| Time | Activity | Details |<br />\n|------|----------|---------|<br />\n| 07:30 – 08:30 | <strong>Breakfast</strong> | <strong>The Nutcracker</strong> (Colaba) – try the masala omelette, fresh fruit, and filter coffee. |<br />\n| 08:45 – 09:30 | <strong>Gateway of India</strong> | Photo stop; enjoy the sea breeze. |<br />\n| 09:45 – 11:30 | <strong>Elephanta Island</strong> | Ferry from the Gateway (≈ 15 min). Explore the 5th‑century rock‑cut caves (≈ 2 h). Return by 11:30. |<br />\n<br />\n### Afternoon  <br />\n<br />\n| Time | Activity | Details |<br />\n|------|----------|---------|<br />\n| 12:15 – 13:30 | <strong>Lunch</strong> | <strong>Trishna</strong> (Fort) – famous for butter garlic crab and Malvani fish curry. |<br />\n| 13:45 – 15:15 | <strong>Heritage Walk – South Bombay</strong> | Guided walk (≈ 1.5 h) covering <strong>Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Terminus</strong>, <strong>Flora Fountain</strong>, <strong>St. Thomas Cathedral</strong>, and <strong>Prince of Wales Museum</strong> (now Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj Vastu Sangrahalaya). |<br />\n| 15:30 – 16:30 | <strong>Coffee break</strong> | <strong>Leopold Café</strong> (Colaba) – historic hangout; try the Irani chai. |<br />\n<br />\n### Evening  <br />\n<br />\n| Time | Activity | Details |<br />\n|------|----------|---------|<br />\n| 17:00 – 18:30 | <strong>Marine Drive Sunset</strong> | Walk the promenade, watch the “Queen’s Necklace” lights come on. |<br />\n| 19:00 – 20:30 | <strong>Dinner</strong> | <strong>The Table</strong> (Colaba) – contemporary global cuisine with Indian twists; recommend the pork belly or paneer tikka. |<br />\n| 21:00 – 22:30 | <strong>Nightcap / Cultural Show</strong> | <strong>NCPA (National Centre for the Performing Arts)</strong> – check schedule for a short classical music or dance performance. If unavailable, enjoy a cocktail at <strong>Aer Bar &amp; Lounge</strong> (Four Seasons, Worli) for city skyline views. |<br />\n<br />\n### Travel &amp; Transfers  <br />\n<br />\n- <strong>Airport/railway station → hotel</strong>: Pre‑book a <strong>premium sedan (e.g., Meru or Uber Premier)</strong> – ~₹ 1,200‑₹ 1,500 from Chhatrapati Shivaji International Airport to South Bombay.  <br />\n- <strong>In‑city travel</strong>: Use the same sedan for the day or rely on <strong>auto‑rickshaws</strong> for short hops (₹ 30‑₹ 80).  <br />\n- <strong>Ferry to Elephanta</strong>: Ticket ₹ 200 (Adult) + ₹ 30 (boat fee). Ferries run every 30 min (subject to tide).  <br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 4️⃣ ACCOMMODATION (Optional – if you need a place to stay the night)<br />\n<br />\n| Hotel | Location | Facilities | Approx. price per night* |<br />\n|-------|----------|------------|--------------------------|<br />\n| <strong>The Taj Mahal Palace</strong> | Colaba (near Gateway) | Heritage luxury, sea‑view rooms, spa, multiple dining venues, rooftop pool | ₹ 25,000‑₹ 35,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Four Seasons Hotel Mumbai</strong> | Worli | Contemporary design, infinity pool, spa, fine‑dining, city‑view rooms | ₹ 22,000‑₹ 30,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Trident, Nariman Point</strong> | Nariman Point | Business‑friendly, rooftop bar, sea‑view, fitness centre | ₹ 15,000‑₹ 22,000 |<br />\n<br />\n\\*Prices are <strong>estimates</strong> for a standard king‑room (incl. taxes) based on 2024 rates; actual rates vary by date and availability.  <br />\n<br />\n*If you only need a day‑use lounge, many luxury hotels offer <strong>day‑use rooms</strong> (≈₹ 5,000‑₹ 8,000) for freshening up and a quick nap.*<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 5️⃣ FOOD &amp; EXPERIENCES  <br />\n<br />\n| Meal | Recommendation | Must‑try items |<br />\n|------|----------------|----------------|<br />\n| <strong>Breakfast</strong> | The Nutcracker (Colaba) | Masala omelette, fresh juices |<br />\n| <strong>Street snack</strong> | Crawford Market stalls | Vada pav, pani puri, bhaji pav |<br />\n| <strong>Lunch</strong> | Trishna (Fort) | Butter garlic crab, Malvani fish curry |<br />\n| <strong>Afternoon tea</strong> | Leopold Café (Colaba) | Irani chai, bun maska |<br />\n| <strong>Dinner</strong> | The Table (Colaba) | Pork belly, paneer tikka, seasonal tasting menu |<br />\n| <strong>Nightcap</strong> | Aer Bar (Worli) | Signature cocktail “Mumbai Skyline” |<br />\n<br />\n<strong>Cultural tip:</strong> Ask the server for “<strong>Maharashtrian thali</strong>” – a balanced plate of rotis, bhakri, dal, vegetable, and a sweet. It’s a quick way to sample local flavors.<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 6️⃣ ESTIMATED BUDGET  <br />\n<br />\n| Category | Approx. cost (per person) |<br />\n|----------|---------------------------|<br />\n| <strong>Accommodation</strong> (optional night‑stay) | ₹ 15,000 – ₹ 35,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Transport</strong> (airport‑hotel, sedan for the day) | ₹ 1,200 – ₹ 2,000 |<br />\n| <strong>Ferry to Elephanta</strong> | ₹ 230 |<br />\n| <strong>Meals</strong> (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snacks) | ₹ 2,500 – ₹ 3,500 |<br />\n| <strong>Activities</strong> (guided heritage walk, NCPA ticket) | ₹ 500 – ₹ 1,200 |<br />\n| <strong>Miscellaneous</strong> (tips, small purchases) | ₹ 300 – ₹ 600 |<br />\n| <strong>Total (excluding optional hotel)</strong> | <strong>₹ 4,730 – ₹ 7,530</strong> (≈ US$ 58‑92) |<br />\n| <strong>Total with luxury night‑stay</strong> | <strong>₹ 19,730 – ₹ 42,530</strong> (≈ US$ 240‑520) |<br />\n<br />\n*Assumptions:* Solo traveler, mid‑range dining, standard sedan, no major shopping splurges.<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 7️⃣ IMPORTANT TRAVEL TIPS  <br />\n<br />\n- <strong>Weather &amp; clothing</strong>: Light cotton or linen shirts, comfortable walking shoes, a light sweater for evenings (especially on the sea front). Carry a small <strong>umbrella</strong> or rain‑coat if traveling in monsoon‑transition months (June‑September).  <br />\n- <strong>Transport</strong>: Book a <strong>trusted sedan</strong> (Meru, Savaari, or Uber Premier) in advance; traffic can be heavy during 8‑10 am and 5‑8 pm.  <br />\n- <strong>Reservations</strong>: <strong>Highly recommended</strong> for lunch at Trishna and dinner at The Table—both fill quickly, especially on weekends.  <br />\n- <strong>Safety</strong>: Mumbai is generally safe for tourists, but keep an eye on belongings in crowded markets and on public transport. Use <strong>registered cabs</strong> only.  <br />\n- <strong>Documents</strong>: Carry a <strong>photocopy of your passport/ID</strong>, hotel confirmation, and a <strong>digital copy of your travel insurance</strong>.  <br />\n- <strong>Currency</strong>: Indian Rupee (₹). ATMs are ubiquitous; most upscale restaurants accept credit cards, but small street stalls prefer cash.  <br />\n- <strong>Health</strong>: Drink bottled water, avoid raw salads from street vendors, and consider a <strong>basic travel health kit</strong> (hand sanitizer, motion sickness tablets).  <br />\n<br />\nEnjoy your whirlwind day in Mumbai—where colonial grandeur, bustling markets, and the Arabian Sea converge for an unforgettable urban adventure!', 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1476514525535-07fb3b4ae5f1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80', '2026-11-19 08:13:46'),
(36, 'vdholariya623@rku.ac.in', 'user', 'trip of rajastan 18 days', NULL, '2026-11-19 08:16:04'),
(37, 'vdholariya623@rku.ac.in', 'ai', '## 1. TRIP OVERVIEW  <br />\n<br />\n| Item | Details |<br />\n|------|---------|<br />\n| <strong>Destination</strong> | Rajasthan, India – covering Jaipur, Ajmer‑Pushkar, Jodhpur, Jaisalmer, Bikaner, Udaipur, Ranakpur &amp; Mount Abu |<br />\n| <strong>Duration</strong> | 18 days / 17 nights |<br />\n| <strong>Best travel period</strong> | Late October – early March (cool, dry weather; peak season for festivals and desert activities) |<br />\n| <strong>Estimated budget</strong> | <strong>Luxury</strong>: US $5,500 – $7,500 per person  &lt;br&gt;<strong>Mid‑range</strong>: US $3,200 – $4,200 per person  &lt;br&gt;*(see detailed breakdown below)* |<br />\n| <strong>Travel style</strong> | Mix of heritage palace stays, boutique heritage hotels, desert camp nights, and wellness moments in lakeside Udaipur. |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 2. DESTINATION HIGHLIGHTS  <br />\n<br />\n| Category | Highlights |<br />\n|----------|------------|<br />\n| <strong>Real attractions</strong> | Amber Fort, City Palace (Jaipur); Ajmer Sharif Dargah; Pushkar Brahma Temple &amp; lake; Mehrangarh Fort (Jodhpur); Umaid Bhawan Palace; Jaisalmer Fort &amp; Patwon Ki Haveli; Bikaner’s Junagarh Fort; Lake Pichola (Udaipur); Ranakpur Jain Temple; Dilwara Temples (Mount Abu) |<br />\n| <strong>Landscapes &amp; landmarks</strong> | Thar Desert dunes, Aravalli hills, Pushkar sand dunes, Banas River, Kumbhalgarh Fort’s massive wall, Nakki Lake (Mount Abu) |<br />\n| <strong>Wellness experiences</strong> | Ayurvedic spa at Taj Lake Palace (Udaipur), yoga at the desert camp in Jaisalmer, heritage‑style hammam at Samode Haveli (Jaipur) |<br />\n| <strong>Local activities</strong> | Elephant ride at Amber Fort, camel safari &amp; night under the stars in Sam Sagar (Jaisalmer), folk‑dance shows (Kalbeliya in Jodhpur), cooking class for Rajasthani thali, silk‑weaving workshop in Bikaner, boat ride on Lake Pichola |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 3. DAY‑BY‑DAY ITINERARY  <br />\n<br />\n&gt; <strong>Travel notes</strong> – All internal flights are short (Jaipur‑Jodhpur, Jodhpur‑Udaipur) and can be replaced with first‑class rail (Rajdhani or Palace on Wheels) if you prefer. Private‑car transfers with a driver are the most comfortable for the road‑bound legs.  <br />\n<br />\n| Day | Morning | Afternoon | Evening | Transfer / Travel |<br />\n|-----|---------|-----------|---------|-------------------|<br />\n| <strong>1</strong> | Arrive in <strong>Jaipur</strong> – check‑in at <strong>Taj Jai Mahal</strong> (heritage palace). | Guided city walk: Hawa Mahal, Jantar Mantar, local market. | Welcome Rajasthani dinner with folk dance at the hotel. | – |<br />\n| <strong>2</strong> | Amber Fort &amp; Jaigarh Fort with an <strong>elephant‑optional</strong> guided tour. | Lunch at <strong>1135 AD</strong> (inside Amber Fort). | Sunset at Nahargarh Fort – panoramic city view. | – |<br />\n| <strong>3</strong> | Drive to <strong>Ajmer</strong> (≈2 h). Visit Ajmer Sharif Dargah. | Continue to <strong>Pushkar</strong> (≈30 min). Explore Brahma Temple &amp; Pushkar Lake. | Sunset camel ride on the sand dunes. | Private car; overnight at <strong>Pushkar Bagh</strong> (heritage resort). |<br />\n| <strong>4</strong> | Leisure morning – optional yoga by the lake. | Return to Jaipur. Check‑in at <strong>Samode Haveli</strong> (boutique heritage). | Evening cultural program at the haveli. | Private car (≈5 h). |<br />\n| <strong>5</strong> | Fly to <strong>Jodhpur</strong> (≈1 h). Check‑in at <strong>Umaid Bhawan Palace</strong> (luxury). | Guided tour of Mehrangarh Fort &amp; Jaswant Thada. | Dinner at <strong>Indique</strong> (palace rooftop). | – |<br />\n| <strong>6</strong> | Day‑trip to <strong>Jaisalmer</strong> (≈5 h drive). Check‑in at <strong>Desert Camp – Sam Sagar</strong> (luxury tented camp). | Sunset camel safari on the dunes. | Star‑gazing with a Rajasthani astronomer. | Road transfer; overnight in desert camp. |<br />\n| <strong>7</strong> | Sunrise over the dunes, breakfast in the camp. | Explore <strong>Jaisalmer Fort</strong>, Patwon Ki Haveli, Gadisar Lake. | Evening cultural show at the camp. | – |<br />\n| <strong>8</strong> | Drive to <strong>Bikaner</strong> (≈6 h). Check‑in at <strong>Laxmi Niwas Palace</strong> (heritage hotel). | Visit Junagarh Fort &amp; the <strong>National Research Centre on Camel</strong>. | Dinner with Bikaneri bhujia tasting. | – |<br />\n| <strong>9</strong> | Morning silk‑weaving workshop in Bikaner. | Fly to <strong>Udaipur</strong> (≈1 h). Check‑in at <strong>Taj Lake Palace</strong> (floating palace). | Boat ride on Lake Pichola at sunset. | – |<br />\n| <strong>10</strong> | Guided tour: City Palace, Saheliyon‑Ki‑Bari, Jagdish Temple. | Lunch at <strong>Upre</strong> (lake‑front). | Evening at <strong>Bagore‑Ki‑Haveli</strong> cultural show. | – |<br />\n| <strong>11</strong> | Day‑trip to <strong>Ranakpur</strong> (≈2 h). Visit the 1,400‑pillar Jain Temple. | Return via <strong>Kumbhalgarh Fort</strong> – walk part of the 36 km wall. | Overnight at <strong>The Fort Kumbhalgarh</strong> (heritage resort). | Road transfer. |<br />\n| <strong>12</strong> | Morning trek along Kumbhalgarh wall. | Return to Udaipur. Spa treatment at <strong>Taj Lake Palace</strong>. | Free evening – explore the lakeside market. | – |<br />\n| <strong>13</strong> | Fly to <strong>Mount Abu</strong> (≈1 h). Check‑in at <strong>The Palace Hotel</strong> (heritage). | Visit Dilwara Temples &amp; Nakki Lake. | Sunset at <strong>Sunset Point</strong>. | – |<br />\n| <strong>14</strong> | Leisure morning – optional trekking in the Aravalli hills. | Return to Jaipur (flight ≈1 h). Check‑in at <strong>Rambagh Palace</strong> (luxury). | Private dinner in the palace garden. | – |<br />\n| <strong>15</strong> | Day‑trip to <strong>Alwar</strong> &amp; <strong>Bharatpur</strong> (Keoladeo National Park – bird sanctuary). | Safari in the park (guided). | Return to Jaipur. | Private car (≈3 h each way). |<br />\n| <strong>16</strong> | Shopping &amp; leisure in Jaipur – Johari Bazaar, Bapu Bazaar. | Optional cooking class (Rajasthani thali). | Farewell dinner at <strong>Suvarna Mahal</strong> (Rambagh Palace). | – |<br />\n| <strong>17</strong> | Transfer to <strong>Jaipur International Airport</strong> for departure. | – | – | – |<br />\n| <strong>18</strong> | <strong>Travel day</strong> – arrival home. | – | – | – |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 4. ACCOMMODATION  <br />\n<br />\n| City | Property (verified) | Location | Facilities | Approx. price per night (USD) |<br />\n|------|---------------------|----------|------------|------------------------------|<br />\n| Jaipur | <strong>Taj Jai Mahal</strong> | Near City Palace | Rooftop pool, spa, heritage rooms, fine‑dining | $300‑$400 (luxury) |<br />\n| Jaipur | <strong>Samode Haveli</strong> | Old City | Courtyard pool, rooftop restaurant, heritage décor | $250‑$320 |<br />\n| Jodhpur | <strong>Umaid Bhawan Palace</strong> | Near Mehrangarh Fort | Golf course, spa, multiple restaurants, museum | $350‑$500 |<br />\n| Jaisalmer | <strong>Sam Sagar Desert Camp</strong> | Sam Sagar dunes (≈30 km from city) | Luxury tents, private baths, desert‑spa, camel safaris | $250‑$350 |<br />\n| Bikaner | <strong>Laxmi Niwas Palace</strong> | Near Junagarh Fort | Heritage rooms, pool, rooftop restaurant | $180‑$250 |<br />\n| Udaipur | <strong>Taj Lake Palace</strong> | On Lake Pichola | Floating palace, spa, fine‑dining, private boat rides | $500‑$650 |<br />\n| Udaipur | <strong>The Fort Kumbhalgarh</strong> | Near Kumbhalgarh Fort | Heritage rooms, pool, yoga pavilion | $180‑$240 |<br />\n| Mount Abu | <strong>The Palace Hotel</strong> | Near Nakki Lake | Heritage building, garden, rooftop restaurant | $150‑$200 |<br />\n| Jaipur (final nights) | <strong>Rambagh Palace</strong> | Near Jaipur Zoo | Luxury palace, spa, multiple dining venues | $450‑$600 |<br />\n<br />\n*Prices are <strong>estimates</strong> for the 2024‑2025 season, based on publicly listed rates for standard rooms. Peak‑season (Dec‑Jan) can be 15‑20 % higher. All properties are verified on their official websites.*<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 5. FOOD &amp; EXPERIENCES  <br />\n<br />\n| Experience | Description |<br />\n|------------|-------------|<br />\n| <strong>Rajasthani thali</strong> | Multi‑dish platter (dal baati churma, gatte ki sabzi, ker sangri, sweets) – try at <strong>Chokhi Dhani</strong> (Jaipur) or <strong>Upre</strong> (Udaipur). |<br />\n| <strong>Street‑food tour</strong> | Jaipur’s <strong>Jal Mahal</strong> area for kachori, samosa, lassi; Jodhpur’s <strong>Sardar Market</strong> for pyaaz ki kachori. |<br />\n| <strong>Royal dinner</strong> | At <strong>Suvarna Mahal</strong> (Rambagh Palace) – multi‑course menu with live folk music. |<br />\n| <strong>Cooking class</strong> | 3‑hour Rajasthani thali workshop at <strong>Moti Mahal</strong> (Jaipur). |<br />\n| <strong>Camel safari &amp; desert dinner</strong> | Night under the stars at Sam Sagar camp – includes a traditional Rajasthani BBQ (dal baati, kebabs, folk songs). |<br />\n| <strong>Cultural performances</strong> | Evening folk‑dance shows at <strong>Bagore‑Ki‑Haveli</strong> (Udaipur) and <strong>Kalbeliya</strong> dance in Jodhpur. |<br />\n| <strong>Wellness</strong> | Ayurvedic massage at <strong>Taj Lake Palace</strong> spa; yoga session at sunrise in the desert camp. |<br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 6. ESTIMATED BUDGET  <br />\n<br />\n| Category | Luxury (USD) | Mid‑range (USD) |<br />\n|----------|--------------|-----------------|<br />\n| <strong>Accommodation</strong> (16 nights) | $7,200 – $9,600 | $3,200 – $4,800 |<br />\n| <strong>Domestic flights / charter</strong> | $800 – $1,200 | $500 – $800 |<br />\n| <strong>Private‑car transfers &amp; driver</strong> | $1,200 – $1,500 | $800 – $1,100 |<br />\n| <strong>Food (incl. fine‑dining &amp; local eateries)</strong> | $1,200 – $1,500 | $800 – $1,200 |<br />\n| <strong>Activities &amp; entry fees</strong> (forts, desert camp, spa, cultural shows) | $800 – $1,000 | $500 – $700 |<br />\n| <strong>Miscellaneous</strong> (tips, souvenirs, travel insurance) | $300 – $500 | $200 – $350 |<br />\n| <strong>Total per person</strong> | <strong>$11,500 – $14,600</strong> | <strong>$6,000 – $8,550</strong> |<br />\n<br />\n<strong>Assumptions</strong><br />\n<br />\n* Solo traveler; shared‑room rates applied for mid‑range estimate.  <br />\n* Prices quoted in USD, based on 2024‑2025 rates.  <br />\n* International airfare not included.  <br />\n* Travel insurance at US $150 – $250 (not shown).  <br />\n<br />\n---<br />\n<br />\n## 7. IMPORTANT TRAVEL TIPS  <br />\n<br />\n| Topic | Advice |<br />\n|-------|--------|<br />\n| <strong>Weather &amp; clothing</strong> | Daytime 15‑30 °C (cool mornings/evenings). Pack light layers, a warm sweater for desert nights, comfortable walking shoes, and a modest scarf for temple visits. |<br />\n| <strong>Transport</strong> | Hire a reputable <strong>AC sedan with driver</strong> for road legs; they handle parking and local permits. Domestic flights (Jaipur‑Jodhpur, Jodhpur‑Udaipur, Udaipur‑Mount Abu) are short and save time. |<br />\n| <strong>Reservations</strong> | Book palace hotels <strong>6‑9 months</strong> ahead for peak season. Desert camp slots fill quickly; reserve at least 4 months ahead. |<br />\n| <strong>Safety</strong> | Rajasthan is generally safe for tourists. Keep valuables in hotel safes, use bottled water, and avoid isolated desert stretches after dark unless with a guided group. |<br />\n| <strong>Documents</strong> | Indian visa (e‑tourist) required for most nationalities. Carry a printed copy of your passport, visa, and travel insurance. |<br />\n| <strong>Health</strong> | No mandatory vaccines, but <strong>hep‑A, typhoid, and routine shots</strong> are recommended. Carry a basic medical kit; altitude is not an issue. |<br />\n| <strong>Cultural etiquette</strong> | Remove shoes before entering temples and private homes. Dress modestly (shoulders and knees covered) when visiting religious sites. |<br />\n| <strong>Currency</strong> | Indian Rupee (INR). ATMs widely', NULL, '2026-11-19 08:16:12'),
